### a) Associativity of an Operator (3 Marks)

- **Definition:** **Associativity** specifies the **direction of grouping** (`Left-to-Right` or `Right-to-Left`) when two or more operators of the **same precedence level** appear adjacent in an unparenthesized expression.
- **Precedence vs Associativity:** Precedence decides between *different* levels (`*` binds tighter than `+` in `2 + 3 * 4 = 14`), whereas associativity breaks ties *within the same level*:

| Associativity Direction | C Operators in This Group | Grouping Example |
|---|---|---|
| **Left-to-Right (`L -> R`)** | `* / %`, `+ -`, `< <= > >=`, `== !=`, `&&`, `` `||` `` | `20 - 8 - 3` groups as `(20 - 8) - 3 = 9` (not `20 - 5 = 15`). |
| **Right-to-Left (`R -> L`)** | Unary `! ~ ++ -- - * & sizeof`, ternary `?:`, assignment `= += -=` | `a = b = 5` groups as `a = (b = 5)`, assigning `5` to `b` then `a`. |

### b) `break` and `continue` Statements (3 Marks)

> [!COMPARE] `break` vs `continue` in C
> | Parameter | `break` Statement | `continue` Statement |
> |---|---|---|
> | **Action** | Immediately **terminates** the innermost enclosing loop or `switch`. | **Skips only the rest of the current iteration** and starts the next cycle. |
> | **Valid Contexts** | Inside `for`, `while`, `do-while`, and `switch`. | Inside loops (`for`, `while`, `do-while`) **only** (invalid in `switch`). |
> | **Control Transfer** | Jumps to the first statement **after** the loop/`switch` block. | Jumps to `update` (`for`) or loop `condition` (`while` / `do-while`). |

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    for (int i = 1; i <= 6; i++) {
        if (i == 3) {
            continue; /* skips printing 3, continues with i = 4 */
        }
        if (i == 5) {
            break;    /* exits loop completely when i reaches 5 */
        }
        printf("%d ", i);
    }
    printf("\n");
    return 0;
}
```

- **Output:** `1 2 4` (`3` is skipped by `continue`; the loop halts before printing `5` or `6` due to `break`).

> [!MARKS] 3 + 3 = 6 Marks
> **a) GCD Program (`3m`):** Euclidean division algorithm `gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a % b)` + sample trace | **b) Avoiding `goto` (`3m`):** Four structured-programming reasons + preferred alternatives.

### a) C Program to Calculate GCD of Two Numbers (3 Marks)

> [!FORMULA] Euclid's Division Identity
> `gcd(a, 0) = |a|` and `gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a mod b)` for `b != 0` (runs in `O(log(min(a, b)))` steps).

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int findGCD(int a, int b) {
    if (a < 0) a = -a;
    if (b < 0) b = -b;
    while (b != 0) {
        int rem = a % b;
        a = b;
        b = rem;
    }
    return a;
}

int main(void) {
    int x, y;
    printf("Enter two integers: ");
    if (scanf("%d %d", &x, &y) != 2) {
        return 1;
    }
    printf("GCD of %d and %d = %d\n", x, y, findGCD(x, y));
    return 0;
}
```

- **Dry-Run Trace (`x = 48, y = 18`):** `(48, 18) -> 48 % 18 = 12` -> `(18, 12) -> 18 % 12 = 6` -> `(12, 6) -> 12 % 6 = 0` -> returns **`6`**.

### b) Why `goto` Should Be Avoided in C (3 Marks)

The unconditional **`goto label;`** jump is strongly discouraged in structured C programming for four reasons:

- **1. Creates "Spaghetti Code":** Arbitrary forward and backward jumps tangle execution paths, making control flow hard for humans to read and trace.
- **2. Harder Debugging & Verification:** Because any statement with a label can be reached from multiple `goto` sites, reasoning about variable values and loop invariants becomes error-prone.
- **3. Bypassed Variable Initializations:** Jumping forward into a block past `int count = 0;` leaves local variables uninitialized with garbage values.
- **4. Inhibits Compiler Optimizations:** Non-reducible control-flow graphs hinder loop vectorization and register allocation.
- **Structured Alternatives:** Use `if-else`, `switch`, `for`/`while`/`do-while`, `break`, `continue`, and early `return` functions instead (reserving `goto` at most for single-exit error cleanup in low-level systems code).

> [!MARKS] 2 + 4 = 6 Marks
> **a) Nested Loop Structure (`2m`):** Definition, `m × n` execution rule, and syntax | **b) Series Program (`4m`):** Nested-loop C program for `1 + (1+2) + ... + (1+2+...+n)` + trace.

### a) Nested Loop Structure (2 Marks)

- **Definition:** A **nested loop** places one loop (**inner loop**) completely inside the body of another loop (**outer loop**).
- **Execution Rule:** For **every single iteration** of the outer loop, the inner loop initializes and runs **all of its iterations** from start to finish. If the outer loop executes `m` times and the inner loop executes `n` times per outer cycle, the inner body runs **`m × n` times** in total.
- **Common Applications:** Printing 2D star/number patterns, traversing 2D matrices (`mat[i][j]`), and evaluating bracketed triangular series.

### b) C Program for `S = 1 + (1+2) + (1+2+3) + ...` up to `n` Terms (4 Marks)

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n;
    long totalSum = 0;

    printf("Enter number of terms (n): ");
    if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1 || n <= 0) {
        printf("Please enter a positive integer.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        long termSum = 0;
        for (int j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
            termSum += j;      /* computes (1 + 2 + ... + i) */
        }
        totalSum += termSum;   /* accumulates i-th bracket into total */
    }

    printf("Sum of series up to %d terms = %ld\n", n, totalSum);
    return 0;
}
```

- **Dry-Run Trace (`n = 4`):**
  - `i = 1`: `termSum = 1` -> `totalSum = 1`
  - `i = 2`: `termSum = 1 + 2 = 3` -> `totalSum = 1 + 3 = 4`
  - `i = 3`: `termSum = 1 + 2 + 3 = 6` -> `totalSum = 4 + 6 = 10`
  - `i = 4`: `termSum = 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 = 10` -> `totalSum = 10 + 10 =` **`20`** (matches closed-form tetrahedral formula `n(n + 1)(n + 2) / 6 = 4 × 5 × 6 / 6 = 20`).

