> **Standard `<string.h>` Functions (`3m`):** Syntax, purpose, and return behaviour of `strlen`, `strcpy`, `strncpy`, `strcat`, `strcmp`, `strchr` | **Complete Program (`3m`):** Runnable C program demonstrating string operations.

### 1. Standard String Handling Functions (`<string.h>`) (3 Marks)

All C string library functions operate on null-terminated (`'\0'`) character arrays:

| Function | Prototype Summary | Purpose & Return Value |
|---|---|---|
| **`strlen(s)`** | `size_t strlen(const char *s);` | Counts characters before `'\0'` (**excludes** `'\0'`). `strlen("Code")` -> `4`. |
| **`strcpy(dest, src)`** | `char *strcpy(char *dest, const char *src);` | Copies `src` (including `'\0'`) into mutable array `dest`. |
| **`strncpy(dest, src, n)`** | `char *strncpy(char *dest, const char *src, size_t n);` | Bounded copy of up to `n` characters to prevent buffer overflow. |
| **`strcat(dest, src)`** | `char *strcat(char *dest, const char *src);` | Appends `src` onto the end of mutable `dest`, overwriting `dest`'s old `'\0'`. |
| **`strcmp(s1, s2)`** | `int strcmp(const char *s1, const char *s2);` | Lexicographical ASCII comparison: returns `0` if equal, `< 0` if `s1 < s2`, `> 0` if `s1 > s2`. |
| **`strchr(s, ch)`** | `char *strchr(const char *s, int ch);` | Returns pointer to first occurrence of `ch` in `s` (or `NULL` if absent). |

> [!TRAP] Buffer Capacity Rule
> Never pass a read-only string literal as the first argument to `strcpy()` or `strcat()`—`dest` must be a writable `char` array large enough to hold the combined characters **plus `'\0'`**.

### 2. C Program Demonstrating String Functions (3 Marks)

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(void) {
    char s1[32] = "Diploma";
    char s2[32];

    strcpy(s2, s1);                  /* s2 = "Diploma" */
    printf("Length of s1 = %zu\n", strlen(s1));

    strcat(s1, " in C");             /* s1 = "Diploma in C" */
    printf("After strcat = %s\n", s1);

    if (strcmp(s2, "Diploma") == 0) {
        printf("s2 matches \"Diploma\"\n");
    }
    if (strchr(s1, 'C') != NULL) {
        printf("'C' found at index %td\n", strchr(s1, 'C') - s1);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

- **Output:**
  - `Length of s1 = 7`
  - `After strcat = Diploma in C`
  - `s2 matches "Diploma"`
  - `'C' found at index 11`

