### 1. Four Storage Classes in C (4 Marks)

A variable's **storage class** specifies four properties: **storage location**, **default initial value**, **visibility (scope)**, and **lifetime**:

| Storage Class | Keyword | Storage Location | Default Initial Value | Scope (Visibility) | Lifetime |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Automatic** | `auto` | Runtime Stack | Garbage (indeterminate) | Local to enclosing block `{}` | Created on block entry, destroyed on block exit |
| **Register** | `register` | CPU Register (or Stack fallback) | Garbage (indeterminate) | Local to enclosing block `{}` | Until enclosing block exits (`&var` forbidden) |
| **Static** | `static` | Data / `.bss` Segment | **`0`** (zero) | Local to block (or file-private if global) | **Entire program execution** (retains value across calls) |
| **External** | `extern` | Data / `.bss` Segment | **`0`** (zero) | Global across multiple `.c` files | **Entire program execution** |

- **`auto`:** Implicit default for all local variables (`int x;` inside a function means `auto int x;`).
- **`register`:** Requests fast CPU register placement for heavily accessed loop counters; unary address-of `&` cannot be applied to a `register` variable.
- **`static`:** Initialized **only once**; a local `static` variable preserves its updated state across repeated calls, while a top-level `static` variable restricts linkage to its own `.c` file.
- **`extern`:** Declares a global variable defined in another translation unit without allocating duplicate storage.

### 2. C Program Illustrating Storage Classes (2 Marks)

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int sharedTotal = 100;         /* external/global variable */

void counterDemo(void) {
    auto int localVal = 1;     /* re-initialized every call */
    static int savedCount = 1; /* persists across calls */
    register int step = 1;
    printf("local=%d, static=%d, global=%d\n", localVal, savedCount, sharedTotal);
    localVal += step;
    savedCount += step;
}

int main(void) {
    extern int sharedTotal;
    counterDemo();
    counterDemo();
    counterDemo();
    return 0;
}
```

- **Output:**
  - Call 1: `local=1, static=1, global=100`
  - Call 2: `local=1, static=2, global=100`
  - Call 3: `local=1, static=3, global=100`

> [!MARKS] 4 + 2 = 6 Marks
> **Complete In-Place C Program (`4m`):** Safe `fgets` input + manual length scan + two-pointer symmetric swap | **Working & Complexity Trace (`2m`):** Step-by-step dry run on `"Diploma"`.

### 1. C Program to Reverse a String In-Place (4 Marks)

- **Approach:** Find string length `len` by scanning up to `'\0'`, then use two indices (`left = 0`, `right = len - 1`) moving inward to swap symmetric character pairs without requiring any non-standard library function (`strrev()` is not part of ISO C).

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void reverseString(char s[]) {
    int len = 0;
    while (s[len] != '\0') {
        len++;
    }
    for (int left = 0, right = len - 1; left < right; left++, right--) {
        char temp = s[left];
        s[left] = s[right];
        s[right] = temp;
    }
}

int main(void) {
    char str[100];
    printf("Enter a string: ");
    if (fgets(str, sizeof(str), stdin) == NULL) {
        return 1;
    }

    /* Remove trailing newline read by fgets, if present */
    for (int i = 0; str[i] != '\0'; i++) {
        if (str[i] == '\n') {
            str[i] = '\0';
            break;
        }
    }

    reverseString(str);
    printf("Reversed string: %s\n", str);
    return 0;
}
```

### 2. Step-by-Step Working & Complexity (2 Marks)

- **Dry-Run Trace on `"Diploma"` (`len = 7`, indices `0..6`):**
  1. `left = 0, right = 6`: swap `'D'` and `'a'` -> `"aiplomD"`
  2. `left = 1, right = 5`: swap `'i'` and `'m'` -> `"amploiD"`
  3. `left = 2, right = 4`: swap `'p'` and `'o'` -> `"amolpiD"`
  4. `left = 3, right = 3`: `left < right` is false (`'l'` stays at centre); `'\0'` at index `7` remains untouched -> **`"amolpiD"`**.
- **Complexity:** Performs `len / 2` swaps -> **`O(n)` time** and **`O(1)` auxiliary space**.

> [!MARKS] 2 + 4 = 6 Marks
> **a) Pointer to a Function (`2m`):** Definition, parenthesized syntax `ret (*fp)(args)`, and dispatch use | **b) 1D Array via Pointer (`4m`):** Complete C program using `*(p + i)` + pointer-scaling explanation.

### a) Pointer to a Function (2 Marks)

- **Definition:** A **pointer to a function** holds the entry-point memory address of executable machine code for a function rather than the address of a data variable, enabling runtime function dispatch and callbacks (such as `qsort()` comparators).
- **Declaration Syntax:**

```c
return_type (*fptr)(param_type1, param_type2);
```

- **Parentheses Rule:** Parentheses around `(*fptr)` are mandatory—`int (*fp)(int, int);` declares a **pointer to a function** returning `int`, whereas `int *fp(int, int);` declares a function returning `int *`.
- **Example:** `int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }` -> `int (*fp)(int, int) = add;` -> `int s = fp(4, 6);` (`s = 10`).

### b) C Program to Access a 1D Array Using a Pointer (4 Marks)

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n, sum = 0;
    int arr[50];
    int *p = arr; /* array name decays to &arr[0] */

    printf("Enter number of elements (1-50): ");
    if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1 || n <= 0 || n > 50) {
        return 1;
    }

    printf("Enter %d integers: ", n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        scanf("%d", p + i);       /* p + i == &arr[i] */
    }

    printf("Elements via pointer:\n");
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("arr[%d] = %d (offset +%d)\n", i, *(p + i), i);
        sum += *(p + i);          /* *(p + i) == arr[i] */
    }
    printf("Sum = %d\n", sum);
    return 0;
}
```

- **Pointer Arithmetic Rule:** Adding integer `i` to `int *p` advances the byte address by **`i * sizeof(int)`** bytes (`base + 4*i` on 32/64-bit targets), so `*(p + i)` dereferences `arr[i]` directly.

> [!MARKS] 3 + 3 = 6 Marks
> **a) Recursive Sum of First `25` Natural Numbers (`3m`):** Base case, recurrence `n + sumNatural(n - 1)`, and C program (`= 325`) | **b) Leap Year Program (`3m`):** Complete Gregorian divisibility check + test cases.

### a) Recursive Function for Sum of First `25` Natural Numbers (3 Marks)

> [!FORMULA] Recurrence Relation
> `sumNatural(0) = 0` (base case) and `sumNatural(n) = n + sumNatural(n - 1)` for `n >= 1`.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int sumNatural(int n) {
    if (n <= 0) {
        return 0;                       /* base case terminates recursion */
    }
    return n + sumNatural(n - 1);       /* recursive step */
}

int main(void) {
    int n = 25;
    printf("Sum of first %d natural numbers = %d\n", n, sumNatural(n));
    return 0;
}
```

- **Output & Verification:** `Sum of first 25 natural numbers = 325` (`25 + 24 + ... + 1 + 0 = 25 × 26 / 2 = 325`).

### b) C Program to Check Whether a Year Is a Leap Year (3 Marks)

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int year;
    printf("Enter a year: ");
    if (scanf("%d", &year) != 1 || year <= 0) {
        printf("Invalid year.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    if ((year % 400 == 0) || (year % 4 == 0 && year % 100 != 0)) {
        printf("%d is a Leap Year.\n", year);
    } else {
        printf("%d is NOT a Leap Year.\n", year);
    }
    return 0;
}
```

- **Gregorian Calendar Rule & Test Cases:**
  - Century years (`year % 100 == 0`) are leap years **only if divisible by `400`** (`2000` -> **Leap Year**; `1900` -> **NOT a Leap Year**).
  - Non-century years divisible by `4` are leap years (`2024` -> **Leap Year**; `2023` -> **NOT a Leap Year**).

