> **Complete DMA Program (`4m`):** Runtime `malloc()` allocation + `NULL` check + reading/writing integers via heap pointer + `free()` | **Explanation (`2m`):** Role of `<stdlib.h>`, heap lifecycle, and leak prevention.

### 1. C Program Reading & Writing Integers via Dynamic Memory Allocation (4 Marks)

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(void) {
    int n;
    int *ptr = NULL;

    printf("Enter how many integers to store: ");
    if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1 || n <= 0) {
        printf("Invalid count.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    /* Dynamically allocate contiguous memory for n integers on the heap */
    ptr = (int *)malloc((size_t)n * sizeof(int));
    if (ptr == NULL) {
        printf("Error: Heap memory allocation failed.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    printf("Enter %d integer values:\n", n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        scanf("%d", ptr + i);           /* write into heap block */
    }

    printf("Stored integers read from dynamic memory: ");
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("%d ", *(ptr + i));      /* read back from heap block */
    }
    printf("\n");

    free(ptr);                          /* release heap block */
    ptr = NULL;                         /* avoid dangling pointer */
    return 0;
}
```

### 2. Working of Dynamic Memory Allocation Steps (2 Marks)

- **Runtime Heap Allocation (`malloc`):** Declared in `<stdlib.h>`, `malloc(n * sizeof(int))` requests `n * 4` contiguous bytes from the **heap** at runtime—no fixed compile-time array size is wasted.
- **`NULL`-Check Guard:** If the heap is exhausted, `malloc()` returns `NULL`; checking `if (ptr == NULL)` prevents a null-pointer crash.
- **Pointer Read/Write & Deallocation (`free`):** Values are written to `ptr + i` and read via `*(ptr + i)`. Calling `free(ptr)` returns the block to the heap to prevent **memory leaks**, and setting `ptr = NULL` prevents accidental **dangling-pointer** reuse.
